### `memory.config.*` [RFC v0.4 gap closure]

> **RFC field:** Declared Memory Configuration · **Tier:** MAY · **RFC §:** §10 · **Grounding attacks:** AOC-03 (memory poisoning persistence), TA-05, TA-11, AOC-07 · **ODIS:** `policy_profile_ref`, `constraints`

Memory poisoning is dangerous because it *persists*: an injected instruction written into long-term memory affects sessions the attacker never touches. How long it persists, who can write, and whether writes cross session or tenant boundaries are properties of the declared memory configuration, not of any single memory operation. Without them, a `memory.store` span cannot be assessed for durability of impact. These are configuration-time attributes, tiered **MAY**, and are expected on agent-registration records.

