### `identity.boundary.*` [RFC v0.4 gap closure]

> **RFC field:** Trust-Domain Crossing & Delegation Depth · **Tier:** MUST · **RFC §:** §13 · **Grounding attacks:** AOC-08 (confused deputy), AOC-10, TA-08, TA-12, AOC-15 · **ODIS:** `trust_domain`, `constraints`

The existing `identity.trust.cross_domain` and `identity.delegation.chain_depth` record facts about a single hop. What they do not record is whether *this* hop crossed a boundary the caller was authorised to cross, and how far from the original human principal the current action sits. Confused-deputy attacks work precisely by chaining hops each of which is individually plausible; the crossing itself must therefore be a first-class, explicitly-evaluated event with a decision attached, and the depth must be carried and compared against a declared limit rather than merely counted.

| Attribute | Type | Requirement | Description | Mapping |
|-----------|------|-------------|-------------|---------|
| `identity.boundary.crossed` | boolean | **Required** | Whether this operation crossed a trust-domain boundary. Emit `false` explicitly | NIST AI RMF GOVERN-1.2, OWASP LLM06 (Excessive Agency) |
| `identity.boundary.source_domain` | string | **Required** | Trust domain the request originated in | NIST AI RMF GOVERN-1.2 |
| `identity.boundary.target_domain` | string | **Required** | Trust domain the request entered | NIST AI RMF GOVERN-1.2 |
| `identity.boundary.crossing_type` | string | **Recommended** | Nature of the crossing: `"tenant"`, `"organization"`, `"network_zone"`, `"cloud_account"`, `"partner"`, `"public"` | GDPR Art.32, NIST AI RMF GOVERN-1.2 |
| `identity.boundary.decision` | string | **Required** | Whether the crossing was permitted: `"allowed"`, `"denied"`, `"allowed_with_attenuation"` | OWASP LLM06, NIST AI RMF GOVERN-1.2 |
| `identity.boundary.decision_reason` | string | **Recommended** | Why the crossing was allowed or denied | NIST AI RMF MEASURE-2.5 |
| `identity.boundary.policy_ref` | string | **Recommended** | Policy profile governing the crossing (ODIS `policy_profile_ref`) | NIST AI RMF GOVERN-1.2 |
| `identity.boundary.crossings_count` | int | **Recommended** | Cumulative trust-domain crossings on this request path. Every crossing widens the confused-deputy surface | OWASP LLM06 (Excessive Agency), MITRE ATLAS [AML.T0048](https://atlas.mitre.org/techniques/AML.T0048) |
| `identity.boundary.domains_traversed` | string[] | **Recommended** | Ordered list of trust domains traversed on this path | EU AI Act Art.12 (Record-Keeping) |
| `identity.delegation.depth` | int | **Required** | Number of delegation hops between the original human principal and the current actor. Distinct from `identity.delegation.chain_depth`, which describes one delegation record; `depth` is carried and incremented along the whole request path | OWASP LLM06 (Excessive Agency), EU AI Act Art.14 (Human Oversight) |
| `identity.delegation.max_depth` | int | **Recommended** | Configured maximum permitted delegation depth | OWASP LLM06, NIST AI RMF GOVERN-1.2 |
| `identity.delegation.depth_exceeded` | boolean | **Recommended** | Whether `depth` exceeded `max_depth`. A true value SHOULD be treated as a policy violation, not a warning | OWASP LLM06 (Excessive Agency) |
| `identity.delegation.root_principal` | string | **Required** | The originating human or system principal at the root of the chain. Answers "on whose authority, ultimately?" | EU AI Act Art.14 (Human Oversight), EU AI Act Art.12, NIST AI RMF GOVERN-1.2 |
| `identity.delegation.root_principal_type` | string | **Recommended** | Root principal type: `"human"`, `"service"`, `"scheduled"`, `"external"`, `"unknown"`. `"unknown"` is itself a finding | EU AI Act Art.14, NIST AI RMF GOVERN-1.2 |
| `identity.delegation.root_authenticated_at` | string | **Optional** | RFC 3339 time the root principal last authenticated. A long-lived chain rooted in a stale authentication is a distinct risk | NIST AI RMF GOVERN-1.2 |

**Propagation note (RFC Appendix D.5).** `identity.delegation.depth` and `identity.boundary.crossings_count` must be propagated by a mechanism that is integrity-protected across trust boundaries. They MUST NOT be carried in W3C `baggage`, which any intermediary may rewrite; a token claim, signed header, or protocol-native field is required.

### `identity.credential.mint.*` [RFC v0.4 gap closure]

