### `identity.approval.*` [RFC v0.4 gap closure]

> **RFC field:** Human Approval / Elicitation · **Tier:** MUST · **RFC §:** §14 · **Grounding attacks:** AOC-04 (approval fatigue / spoofed consent), AOC-12, TA-07, AOC-06 · **ODIS:** `owner_ref`, `policy_profile_ref`

`mcp.tool.approval_required` and `mcp.tool.approved` record that approval was needed and given. They do not record *who* approved, *what they were shown*, or *how long they took* — and those are exactly the fields an approval-fatigue or consent-spoofing investigation depends on. An approval granted in 200 milliseconds against a prompt whose text does not match the action performed is the signature of a bypassed control, and no existing attribute makes that visible. Approval is a lifecycle, so it is modelled as an event with a request/decision pair joined by `identity.approval.id`.

| Attribute | Type | Requirement | Description | Mapping |
|-----------|------|-------------|-------------|---------|
| `identity.approval.id` | string | **Required** | Correlation identifier joining the approval request to its decision | EU AI Act Art.12 (Record-Keeping), EU AI Act Art.14 |
| `identity.approval.required` | boolean | **Required** | Whether approval was required for this operation | EU AI Act Art.14 (Human Oversight) |
| `identity.approval.trigger` | string | **Recommended** | Why approval was required: `"policy"`, `"risk_score"`, `"scope_escalation"`, `"first_use"`, `"destructive_action"`, `"elicitation"` | EU AI Act Art.14, NIST AI RMF GOVERN-1.2 |
| `identity.approval.operation` | string | **Required** | The operation being approved, in the same form shown to the approver | EU AI Act Art.14, EU AI Act Art.13 (Transparency) |
| `identity.approval.prompt_hash` | string | **Required** | SHA-256 of the exact text shown to the approver. Comparing this with the action actually performed detects consent substitution | EU AI Act Art.13, MITRE ATLAS [AML.T0051](https://atlas.mitre.org/techniques/AML.T0051) |
| `identity.approval.operation_hash` | string | **Recommended** | SHA-256 of the canonicalised operation as executed. A mismatch with `prompt_hash`'s subject is a finding | EU AI Act Art.14, MITRE ATLAS [AML.T0051](https://atlas.mitre.org/techniques/AML.T0051) |
| `identity.approval.decision` | string | **Required** | Outcome: `"approved"`, `"denied"`, `"timeout"`, `"auto_approved"`, `"bypassed"`, `"cancelled"` | EU AI Act Art.14 (Human Oversight) |
| `identity.approval.approver` | string | **Required** | Identity of the approving principal (ODIS `owner_ref`). MUST be a resolved identity, not a session token | EU AI Act Art.14, EU AI Act Art.12, NIST AI RMF GOVERN-1.2 |
| `identity.approval.approver_type` | string | **Required** | `"human"`, `"policy"`, `"automation"`, `"none"`. `"human"` MUST NOT be used for a rule that auto-approves | EU AI Act Art.14 (Human Oversight) |
| `identity.approval.approver_verified` | boolean | **Recommended** | Whether the approver's identity was authenticated at approval time rather than inherited from an existing session | EU AI Act Art.14, MITRE ATLAS [AML.T0048](https://atlas.mitre.org/techniques/AML.T0048) |
