### `asset.tenant.*` and tenancy scoping [RFC v0.4 gap closure]

> **RFC field:** Organization / Tenant ID · **Tier:** MUST ‡ · **RFC §:** §5 · **Grounding attacks:** AOC-15 (cross-tenant leakage), AOC-03, TA-12, TA-02 · **ODIS:** `owner_ref`, `trust_domain`

Tenancy is the boundary that determines whether an observed event is normal or a breach, and it is cross-cutting: a span that records a retrieval, a memory write, or an agent-to-agent call without a tenant identifier cannot be assessed for isolation failure at all. The RFC marks this **MUST ‡** for that reason — it applies to every content-bearing and every security-relevant signal, not to one span type. The detection half of this pairing lives at [`security.tenant.*`](#securitytenant-rfc-v04-gap-closure); the fields below are the identifiers themselves.

| Attribute | Type | Requirement | Description | Mapping |
|-----------|------|-------------|-------------|---------|
| `asset.tenant.id` | string | **Required** | Tenant the asset belongs to. The primary isolation-boundary identifier | GDPR Art.32, EU AI Act Art.10 (Data Governance), NIST AI RMF GOVERN-1.2 |
| `asset.organization.id` | string | **Required** | Organization owning the asset (ODIS `owner_ref`). May equal `tenant.id` in single-org deployments; MUST still be emitted | NIST AI RMF GOVERN-1.2, EU AI Act Art.12 |
| `asset.organization.name` | string | **Optional** | Human-readable organization name | — |
| `asset.tenant.tier` | string | **Optional** | Tenancy model: `"dedicated"`, `"pooled"`, `"shared_infrastructure"`, `"shared_model"`. Pooled and shared tiers carry materially different isolation assumptions | GDPR Art.32, NIST AI RMF MAP-1.5 |
| `asset.tenant.isolation_boundary` | string | **Recommended** | Where isolation is enforced: `"process"`, `"container"`, `"namespace"`, `"database"`, `"row_level"`, `"logical_only"` | GDPR Art.32, SOC 2 CC6.1 |
| `asset.tenant.data_residency` | string | **Optional** | Required data-residency region for this tenant | GDPR Art.44 (Transfers), EU AI Act Art.10 |
| `gen_ai.agent.tenant.id` | string | **Required** | Tenant the acting agent is operating on behalf of | GDPR Art.32, OWASP LLM02 (Sensitive Information Disclosure) |
| `gen_ai.conversation.tenant.id` | string | **Required** | Tenant that owns the conversation/session. Compare with `gen_ai.agent.tenant.id` to detect crossing | GDPR Art.32, OWASP LLM02 |
| `user.tenant.id` | string | **Recommended** | Tenant of the human principal who initiated the request | GDPR Art.32, EU AI Act Art.12 |
| `gen_ai.data_source.tenant.id` | string | **Recommended** | Tenant that owns the data source being read. A mismatch with `gen_ai.agent.tenant.id` on a retrieval is a cross-tenant read | OWASP LLM08 (Vector and Embedding Weaknesses), GDPR Art.32 |

**Emission rule (cross-cutting ‡).** Tenant identifiers SHOULD be attached as OTel **Resource** attributes where the process serves exactly one tenant, and MUST be attached as span attributes in any multi-tenant process, where the tenant varies per request. Emitting them only at Resource level in a pooled deployment defeats their purpose.

### `asset.capability.*` [RFC v0.4 gap closure]

> **RFC field:** Capability-Set Change Event · **Tier:** MUST · **RFC §:** §12 · **Grounding attacks:** AOC-11 (silent capability expansion), AOC-16, AOC-09, TA-04 · **ODIS:** `constraints`, `resource_indicators`

An agent's capability set — the tools, servers, skills, and scopes it can reach — defines what it is able to do. A change to that set is a change to the blast radius, and it frequently happens without any code change: a server adds a tool, a marketplace updates a skill, a scope is broadened. Point-in-time inventory does not capture it; a *change event* is required, carrying both the before and after sets so that the delta is recoverable from a single record.

